Lab 8: Remote access with a WireGuard VPN (policy 4.7)

Policy requirement
Remote access only through the VPN, encrypted, with no services exposed directly to the internet.

Step 1: install and generate keys on the VPN server

sudo apt install -y wireguard
cd /etc/wireguard
umask 077
wg genkey | tee server.key | wg pubkey > server.pub
wg genkey | tee client1.key | wg pubkey > client1.pub

Step 2: server configuration /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf

[Interface]
Address = 10.8.0.1/24
ListenPort = 51820
PrivateKey = <contents of server.key>

[Peer]
# client1 (a staff laptop)
PublicKey = <contents of client1.pub>
AllowedIPs = 10.8.0.2/32

Step 3: client configuration

[Interface]
Address = 10.8.0.2/24
PrivateKey = <contents of client1.key>

[Peer]
PublicKey = <contents of server.pub>
Endpoint = vpn.example.edu:51820
AllowedIPs = 10.8.0.0/24, 192.168.10.0/24
PersistentKeepalive = 25

Step 4: enable routing, start, and allow the port in the firewall

echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward=1" | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/99-vpn.conf
sudo sysctl --system
sudo ufw allow 51820/udp
sudo systemctl enable --now wg-quick@wg0
sudo wg show

# on the client
sudo wg-quick up wg0
ping 10.8.0.1

Honest limitation
WireGuard authenticates devices by key pair; it has no built-in second factor. The sample policy requires MFA for VPN, so the organization would need a VPN product with MFA integration, or an additional gateway layer. This is a good classroom discussion: the policy can demand something that the chosen technology does not provide. Then the policy, the standard or the tool must change.